#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198592 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\hspace{0.222em}2z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u-1,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}3v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\hspace{0.222em}2z⟩|}_{x=2u-1\\,y=uv\\,z=3v} \\\\  & =⟨2(2u-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{(2u-1)(3v)}{3}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}2(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4u-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-v,\\hspace{0.222em}6v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨4u-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-v,\\hspace{0.222em}6v⟩\\cdot ⟨3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩ \\\\  & =(4u-2)\\cdot 3v+(-v)\\cdot (-6)+6v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =12uv-6v+6v+12uv \\\\  & =24uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\hspace{0.222em}2z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24uv\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}24uv\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[12{u}^{2}v]_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}12v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[6{v}^{2}]_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =6({2}^{2}-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198593 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨z-y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $-2\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u+v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,-2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z-y,\\,2y-z,\\,xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u+v} \\\\  & =⟨u+v-v,\\,2v-(u+v),\\,uv⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,v-u,\\,uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,v-u,\\,uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-1)+(v-u)\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-u-v+u+uv \\\\  & =uv-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z-y,\\,2y-z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(uv-v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}uv-v\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198594 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨z+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩{|}_{z=x-y} \\\\  & =⟨(x-y)+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+(x-y),\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{x+y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{xy}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot (-1)+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x+x+y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)+{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198596 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,0),$ $(2,-1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x-1,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(2(1)-1,1+0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2(2)-1,2+(-1)) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,2) & =(2(2)-1,2+2) \\\\  & =(3,4) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2(1)-1,1+3) \\\\  & =(1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/1d/1d5943d2cf79e7c77f6db3e939242d7e31068f46506fa618abd24b6ac813d32a.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{1}^{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 21\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198602 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({\\color{blue}-P}\\frac{{\\color{blue}∂g}}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{{\\color{blue}∂y}}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198605 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({\\color{blue}-P}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+({\\color{blue}-Q}\\hspace{0.167em})\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198610 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=u+v} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}+2uv+{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2uv)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\color{blue}2uv}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198615 :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩{|}_{z=-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{e}^{x}}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{{e}^{y}}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{1-x-y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({e}^{x})\\cdot (-1)-({e}^{y})\\cdot (-1)+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{x}y+{e}^{y}+y-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+e+1-x-\\frac{1}{2}-1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}-x-\\frac{1}{2}+e)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+ex]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+e-1 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198623 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3x-2y,\\,v=y+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(3x-2y)^{2}(y+2z)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le 3x-2y\\le 3,\\,0\\le y+2z\\le 2,\\,0\\le 2z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3x-2y,\\,v=y+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3x-2y)^{2}(y+2z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{2}v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{2}v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{u}^{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{u=0}^{u=3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (9-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198624 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le 2y+z\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le y-2z\\le 4\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le 2y+z\\le 1,\\,1\\le y-2z\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=2y+z,\\,w=y-2z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le v\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le w\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{5}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[u]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [v]_{-1}^{1}\\cdot [w]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198948 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x + y \\le 3, 0 \\le y - x \\le 2}$.\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n$u = x + y$\nand\n$v = y - x$,\nthe integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∬_{D}(y^{2} - x^{2})dxdy\n$$\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "The substitution maps the region to $0\\le u\\le3$, $0\\le v\\le2$. Solving for the original coordinates gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u-v}2,\\qquad y=\\frac{u+v}2,\n\\qquad\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac12.\n$$\n\nThe integrand becomes $y^2-x^2=(y-x)(y+x)=uv$. Therefore the transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac12\\int_0^3\\int_0^2uv\\,dv\\,du.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198950 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=xy$ and $v=y+x,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le y+x\\le 10\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=y+x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n.\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{y-x}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,\\frac{1}{|y-x|}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}{e}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[v]_{1}^{10}\\,du \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =9[{e}^{u}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =9({e}^{4}-e).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198951 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[u]_{0}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,dv \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198959 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,1\\le x-y\\le 2,\\,1\\le z\\le (x-y)(x+y)\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y,\\,w=\\sqrt{z},\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}3\\sqrt{z}\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y,\\,w=\\sqrt{z}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le {w}^{2}\\le uv\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}3\\sqrt{z}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}3w\\cdot |-w|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}3{w}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{uv}3{w}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198963 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le 2x+y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-1\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le u\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198964 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+z\\le 2,\\,-1\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /6}^{u=\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198965 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=\\sqrt{y}+x,\\,w=\\frac{1}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-(z+1)^{-2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={w}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [-{w}^{-1}]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199007 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{[\\frac{{e}^{{v}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(e-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)[u]_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)(5-1) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233116 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)=8{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y) & =8{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}) \\\\ [({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(2x)(\\operatorname{ln}(y))] & =24{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+2x\\operatorname{ln}(y) & =24{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =24{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(24{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}(y))}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24y-\\frac{2y\\operatorname{ln}(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233117 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3}=8x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3} & =8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ [{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy]-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}) & =8-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8-2xy}{{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233118 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3})+{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{3}\\cdot 2x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{2y}{3x}\\right)}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233174 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=20$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{20+1}}{20+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{21}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{21})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{21}\\cdot 21{x}^{20} \\\\ &= {x}^{20}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233175 :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{17}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=17$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{17}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{17+1}}{17+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{18}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{18})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 18\\,{x}^{17} \\\\ &= {x}^{17}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233176 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{1/4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233177 :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/5+1}}{(\\frac{3}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8/5}}{\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}{x}^{8/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233178 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}={x}^{2/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{2}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/5+1}}{(\\frac{2}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5}{x}^{7/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{2/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233179 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}={x}^{-4/3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/3+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/3}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{1/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233245 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=\\frac{3}{2},$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|\\frac{3}{2}|=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233246 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-5\\operatorname{sin}(3x-\\pi )-3?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=-5,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|-5|=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233247 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}(12x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{cos}(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=12.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233248 :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{8x}{3}+{20}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{8}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{360}^{∘}}{\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}={135}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233276 :question/problem "![](images/95/95c30eab094a24beb6976262167401efa973dbac12781c69d074eb14e12e2743.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{m}+\\mathbf{n}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n:$ \n\n![](images/87/87647211f77776c5f5f740c205fafb150eef1b9d3ebefb82df73a9fdcd953bdb.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=0.5=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $BH=2.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|m+n| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233325 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=t-4,\\,\\,\\,y=1-2{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 4,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $a$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $b$ | $c$ | $-31$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $x=t-4.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=0-4=-4.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=2$ into $y=1-2{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=1-2(2)^{2}=-7.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=1-2{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=1-2(3)^{2}=-17\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+(-7)+(-17)=-28.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233364 :question/problem "Find $a+3b$ if $a=8i-5j$ and $b=-3i+2j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $a+3b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+3b & =(8i-5j)+3(-3i+2j) \\\\  & =8i-5j-9i+6j \\\\  & =(8i-9i)+(-5j+6j) \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233365 :question/problem "Find $3a-2b$ if $a=-2i-2j$ and $b=4i-3j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $3a-2b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-2b & =3(-2i-2j)-2(4i-3j) \\\\  & =-6i-6j-8i+6j \\\\  & =(-6i-8i)+(-6j+6j) \\\\  & =-14i+0j \\\\  & =-14i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233366 :question/problem "![](images/f3/f35a3ad730621023ec86e5727588fc86083ee500b3a72c55ab2808bd290019e9.png)\n\nLet $P(1,2)$ and $Q(-2,1).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=i+2j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=-2i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =(-2i+j)-(i+2j) \\\\  & =-3i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233367 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{b}{|b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233368 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+b & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+5 \\\\ 4+8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2a+b| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $2a+b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(2a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233379 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (x-2)-6\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=-32$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x+32 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233382 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨-3,1⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨4,6⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v} & =⟨-3,1⟩+⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3+4,1+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
